1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,603
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,623 --> 00:00:07,740
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:11,254
This is Lecture 27, the digital factory.

4
00:00:11,274 --> 00:00:17,053
Last time we stood in a garment factory and connected its sewing machines;

5
00:00:17,073 --> 00:00:20,501
today we move to the plant that builds them.

6
00:00:20,901 --> 00:00:23,432
In the background is today's lab,

7
00:00:23,452 --> 00:00:29,230
where hook clearances measured machine by machine at the adjustment station

8
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:33,868
travel over the bus and become a control chart and an alarm.

9
00:00:33,888 --> 00:00:35,568
From order to service,

10
00:00:35,588 --> 00:00:40,361
how does information move through a plant with an ID attached?

11
00:00:41,881 --> 00:00:42,941
Here's the problem.

12
00:00:43,341 --> 00:00:50,388
The adjustment station measures every hook clearance:

13
00:00:50,408 --> 00:01:00,921
mean 0.070 millimetres and sigma 6 microns when stable, tolerance 0.04 to 0.10.

14
00:01:01,321 --> 00:01:04,689
From machine 46 a new hook lot goes in,

15
00:01:04,709 --> 00:01:08,685
ground with a relaxed wheel-dressing interval,

16
00:01:08,705 --> 00:01:12,421
and the clearance drifts 4 microns an hour.

17
00:01:12,821 --> 00:01:17,660
Today an NCR opens only when one machine fails,

18
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:20,968
and a shipment leaves at hour 8.

19
00:01:21,368 --> 00:01:23,291
What are the X-bar limits,

20
00:01:23,311 --> 00:01:28,148
and how different is acting on failures from acting on run rules?

21
00:01:29,668 --> 00:01:33,342
Seven breaks and the overall architecture.

22
00:01:34,862 --> 00:01:38,085
This plant builds 180 machines a day.

23
00:01:38,485 --> 00:01:45,316
An order arrives: 200 WQ-LS-TED, due in ten days.

24
00:01:45,716 --> 00:01:49,865
With information scattered over spreadsheets, paper tickets and test PCs,

25
00:01:49,885 --> 00:01:53,976
it breaks in at least seven places: the low-oil hook copied as standard,

26
00:01:53,996 --> 00:01:57,288
DLC outsourcing missing from MRP, firmware left to memory,

27
00:01:57,308 --> 00:02:01,570
test results filed without serials, a bottleneck stop noticed at shift end,

28
00:02:01,590 --> 00:02:03,454
a growing clearance nobody plots,

29
00:02:03,474 --> 00:02:06,138
and a complaint nobody can trace to a hook lot.

30
00:02:07,658 --> 00:02:14,232
The plant's information system has five layers: equipment and lines;

31
00:02:14,252 --> 00:02:16,268
the unified data bus,

32
00:02:16,288 --> 00:02:23,055
which every machine and program only publishes to and subscribes from;

33
00:02:23,075 --> 00:02:30,522
the historian, storing every message as the single source of truth; ERP, PLM,

34
00:02:30,542 --> 00:02:35,079
MES, QMS and WMS; and dashboards and AI on top.

35
00:02:35,479 --> 00:02:39,592
Two rules: components talk only through the bus,

36
00:02:39,612 --> 00:02:43,295
so one layer can change without the others;

37
00:02:43,315 --> 00:02:48,375
and writes need a person to confirm, with AI only drafting.

38
00:02:49,895 --> 00:02:55,360
These are the bus messages scrolling past, newest on top.

39
00:02:55,760 --> 00:03:01,975
The plant's root topic is wq/sewing, and following 'add,

40
00:03:01,995 --> 00:03:08,100
never change' it adds assembly, test and service areas.

41
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:15,450
Each measurement at the adjustment station publishes a quality.measurement;

42
00:03:15,470 --> 00:03:18,145
besides the value and limits,

43
00:03:18,165 --> 00:03:24,000
data carries a hook_lot field recording which hook lot went in.

44
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:26,860
Every trace later depends on it.

45
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:32,622
Product data and the bottleneck.

46
00:03:34,142 --> 00:03:38,206
WQ-LS has three options, auto-trim T,

47
00:03:38,226 --> 00:03:45,050
electronic stitch length E and low-oil hook D, each on or off:

48
00:03:45,070 --> 00:03:48,362
2 times 2 times 2, 8 variants.

49
00:03:48,762 --> 00:03:58,150
In the ERP, WQ-LS becomes an item template with T, E and D as attributes,

50
00:03:58,170 --> 00:04:03,692
generating eight variants, each with a BOM.

51
00:04:04,092 --> 00:04:09,304
With more options, a configurator builds the BOM by rule.

52
00:04:09,704 --> 00:04:14,457
Sales picks options, MRP sees the DLC outsourcing,

53
00:04:14,477 --> 00:04:17,702
and breaks one and two are closed.

54
00:04:19,222 --> 00:04:24,369
With routings in the MES you can compute load:

55
00:04:24,389 --> 00:04:33,242
machines a day times minutes each, over units times shifts times 480 times OEE.

56
00:04:33,642 --> 00:04:41,213
The horizontal machining centre takes 16 minutes per housing; five units,

57
00:04:41,233 --> 00:04:48,284
two shifts, OEE 60 percent give exactly 180 a day, 100 percent load:

58
00:04:48,304 --> 00:04:49,844
the bottleneck.

59
00:04:50,244 --> 00:04:55,260
At 68 percent OEE the load falls to 88.

60
00:04:55,660 --> 00:05:02,672
Raising OEE elsewhere adds no output, so phase one connects the bottleneck.

61
00:05:04,192 --> 00:05:08,973
Closing the quality loop: control charts and rules.

62
00:05:10,493 --> 00:05:14,473
Watch the measurements play machine by machine,

63
00:05:14,493 --> 00:05:17,793
sixteen hours in about sixteen seconds.

64
00:05:18,193 --> 00:05:21,780
For two hours the dots scatter around 0.070;

65
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:27,682
the yellow band marks the new hook lot, and from there the cloud climbs.

66
00:05:28,082 --> 00:05:32,714
For a long time not one dot crosses the red tolerance line,

67
00:05:32,734 --> 00:05:36,182
so a system acting on failures stays silent.

68
00:05:36,582 --> 00:05:42,602
That's the trouble with drift: every unit passes while the process gets worse.

69
00:05:44,122 --> 00:05:50,166
The right tool is an X-bar chart: subgroups of five machines,

70
00:05:50,186 --> 00:05:52,850
plotting the subgroup mean.

71
00:05:53,250 --> 00:06:02,401
The centre line is 0.070 millimetres and the limits are three sigma over root

72
00:06:02,421 --> 00:06:04,068
n either side,

73
00:06:04,088 --> 00:06:10,976
about 8.0 microns: upper about 0.0780, lower about 0.0620.

74
00:06:11,376 --> 00:06:15,243
Two points: compute them from stable-process data,

75
00:06:15,263 --> 00:06:19,596
and never substitute the tolerance, which is much wider.

76
00:06:21,116 --> 00:06:25,048
Run rules catch a drift before anything fails.

77
00:06:25,448 --> 00:06:31,056
R1, one point beyond 3 sigma, is the slowest; R2,

78
00:06:31,076 --> 00:06:39,902
two of three beyond 2 sigma on one side; R3, four of five beyond 1 sigma; R4,

79
00:06:39,922 --> 00:06:43,003
eight in a row on one side.

80
00:06:43,403 --> 00:06:46,983
More rules, earlier detection, more false alarms.

81
00:06:47,383 --> 00:06:53,399
Alarms go out as ai.alert with the triggering messages as evidence,

82
00:06:53,419 --> 00:06:56,102
not repeated within two hours,

83
00:06:56,122 --> 00:07:01,507
and the AI drafts a freeze proposal for a person to confirm.

84
00:07:03,027 --> 00:07:08,247
Tick R1 through R4 and play it again.

85
00:07:08,647 --> 00:07:17,700
The subgroup means climb step by step past the two-sigma line in the yellow band,

86
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:22,741
and at machine 70, about 3.1 hours, R2 fires.

87
00:07:23,141 --> 00:07:26,765
That's barely an hour after the drift began,

88
00:07:26,785 --> 00:07:29,911
with not one machine out of tolerance.

89
00:07:30,311 --> 00:07:38,895
With R1 alone it takes until machine 100, 4.4 hours; acting only on failures,

90
00:07:38,915 --> 00:07:44,035
the first NCR opens at machine 174, 7.7 hours.

91
00:07:45,555 --> 00:07:53,663
Every hour later means about 22.5 more affected machines, an hour's output.

92
00:07:54,063 --> 00:07:58,033
But more rules aren't free: with R1 to R4,

93
00:07:58,053 --> 00:08:05,254
over 1000 random seeds the chance of at least one false alarm in 16 hours is

94
00:08:05,274 --> 00:08:06,870
about 54 percent,

95
00:08:06,890 --> 00:08:10,480
and frequent false alarms get ignored.

96
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:16,712
After an alarm, contain first: freeze the remaining hooks,

97
00:08:16,732 --> 00:08:24,380
re-inspect suspect machines, open an NCR, then run an 8D for the root cause.

98
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:30,880
Traceability, versions, service and roll-out.

99
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:38,561
After an alarm, follow serials and lots both ways.

100
00:08:38,961 --> 00:08:47,254
Backward to the cause: the alarmed machine carries hook lot H2610-07,

101
00:08:47,274 --> 00:08:53,157
whose work order ran on raceway grinder GRD-H-02,

102
00:08:53,177 --> 00:08:58,338
where the wheel-dressing event explains it.

103
00:08:58,738 --> 00:09:04,854
Forward to the scope: which other machines got the lot, how many hooks remain,

104
00:09:04,874 --> 00:09:06,742
and whether any shipped.

105
00:09:07,142 --> 00:09:12,376
Without scanning every machine you can only bound the scope by time,

106
00:09:12,396 --> 00:09:13,922
several times wider.

107
00:09:15,442 --> 00:09:21,662
Swap the sewing machine for a six-axis robot and almost everything carries over.

108
00:09:22,062 --> 00:09:24,658
Each joint's reducer lot,

109
00:09:24,678 --> 00:09:31,043
motor and encoder serials are scanned and bound to the robot;

110
00:09:31,063 --> 00:09:37,322
repeatability and backlash go out as test.result with units.

111
00:09:37,722 --> 00:09:40,951
Measure backlash on every robot,

112
00:09:40,971 --> 00:09:48,973
and an X-bar chart with run rules spots a drifting reducer lot before failures,

113
00:09:48,993 --> 00:09:52,222
then bounds the affected robots.

114
00:09:52,622 --> 00:09:55,482
The AI only drafts the freeze.

115
00:09:57,002 --> 00:10:00,042
The traceability everyone knows is a milk recall.

116
00:10:00,442 --> 00:10:04,417
When a supermarket finds a problem with one batch,

117
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:10,490
it recalls by the lot number printed on the carton instead of clearing every

118
00:10:10,510 --> 00:10:12,328
carton off the shelves.

119
00:10:12,728 --> 00:10:18,283
That works only because each carton got a lot number at production and the

120
00:10:18,303 --> 00:10:21,221
plant recorded which stores it went to.

121
00:10:21,621 --> 00:10:27,131
Scanning the hook lot into each machine at assembly is printing a lot number

122
00:10:27,151 --> 00:10:28,877
on every sewing machine.

123
00:10:29,277 --> 00:10:36,259
Without it, when something goes wrong you can only bound the problem by time,

124
00:10:36,279 --> 00:10:37,532
in big blocks.

125
00:10:39,052 --> 00:10:40,152
Back to our problem.

126
00:10:40,552 --> 00:10:47,339
The limits are 0.070 plus or minus 8.0 microns.

127
00:10:47,739 --> 00:10:56,433
After 4, 5 and 6 hours a machine fails with probability about 1.0,

128
00:10:56,453 --> 00:10:58,941
4.8 and 16 percent.

129
00:10:59,341 --> 00:11:11,130
Acting on failures, the first appears at machine 174, 7.7 hours: 140 affected,

130
00:11:11,150 --> 00:11:12,947
135 shipped.

131
00:11:13,347 --> 00:11:22,464
With R1 to R4 the alarm comes at machine 70: 36 affected, all in the plant,

132
00:11:22,484 --> 00:11:25,023
and 264 hooks frozen.

133
00:11:26,543 --> 00:11:32,474
Now Virtual lab 27-1, quality loop and traceability in the digital factory,

134
00:11:32,494 --> 00:11:38,505
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. The results are blurred. On page one,

135
00:11:38,525 --> 00:11:42,790
the left column shows the readings machine by machine,

136
00:11:42,810 --> 00:11:45,726
the X-bar chart and the bus messages;

137
00:11:45,746 --> 00:11:51,599
on the right you pick run rules and set the conditions. Reset to standard,

138
00:11:51,619 --> 00:11:57,391
press play, and note the alarm's machine number, time and affected count.

139
00:11:57,791 --> 00:12:02,118
Click 'New random numbers': for the same rules,

140
00:12:02,138 --> 00:12:06,465
the alarm time and false-alarm count both move,

141
00:12:06,485 --> 00:12:12,568
so the rules you choose must not just look good on one lucky seed.

142
00:12:12,968 --> 00:12:16,405
Now tick R2, R3 and R4 as well and replay.

143
00:12:16,425 --> 00:12:19,450
See how much earlier the alarm comes,

144
00:12:19,470 --> 00:12:25,622
and note how many false alarms the no-drift control gives in sixteen hours.

145
00:12:26,022 --> 00:12:29,642
Last, try R0 alone, the existing 'near the limit' rule,

146
00:12:29,662 --> 00:12:33,614
and think about why it false-alarms so often here. Task one:

147
00:12:33,634 --> 00:12:37,851
find a rule set that alarms at least four hours before the first

148
00:12:37,871 --> 00:12:39,505
out-of-tolerance machine,

149
00:12:39,525 --> 00:12:42,352
with no false alarms when there's no drift.

150
00:12:42,372 --> 00:12:45,661
The guide and report template are on the platform.

151
00:12:47,181 --> 00:12:50,227
This is the WenQuest digital factory,

152
00:12:50,247 --> 00:12:54,784
where today's rules join up into one line. On page two,

153
00:12:54,804 --> 00:13:00,584
the right side is the machine record. Drag the serial number: variant,

154
00:13:00,604 --> 00:13:06,550
work order, hook lot, housing heat number, control-box serial, firmware,

155
00:13:06,570 --> 00:13:13,096
parameter and event-table versions, hook clearance, hook timing and final test.

156
00:13:13,116 --> 00:13:16,659
Every line is one message in the historian.

157
00:13:17,059 --> 00:13:21,826
The graph on the left starts from the machine that alarmed and goes backward

158
00:13:21,846 --> 00:13:22,834
to the hook lot,

159
00:13:22,854 --> 00:13:26,046
the work order, the grinder and the wheel dressing,

160
00:13:26,066 --> 00:13:30,833
then forward to every machine built with that lot, the hooks left in stores,

161
00:13:30,853 --> 00:13:35,746
and anything shipped. Task two is to work out the number of affected machines,

162
00:13:35,766 --> 00:13:37,572
so those figures are blurred.

163
00:13:37,972 --> 00:13:42,281
Page three is the roll-out calculator: investment,

164
00:13:42,301 --> 00:13:46,956
duration and KPI targets for each of the three phases,

165
00:13:46,976 --> 00:13:51,111
with sixty months of cumulative cash flow above.

166
00:13:51,511 --> 00:13:57,034
Set key-equipment OEE to 64 percent and the payback slips. Task three:

167
00:13:57,054 --> 00:14:02,497
keep OEE at 64, make no other KPI or investment better than standard,

168
00:14:02,517 --> 00:14:07,564
and shorten only the phase durations, at least four months each,

169
00:14:07,584 --> 00:14:13,028
to bring payback back within 36 months. How you schedule it, and why,

170
00:14:13,048 --> 00:14:14,611
goes in your report.

171
00:14:16,131 --> 00:14:19,405
The assignment has four questions. One:

172
00:14:19,425 --> 00:14:25,570
the horizontal machining centre takes 16 minutes per housing, five units,

173
00:14:25,590 --> 00:14:30,807
two 480-minute shifts, and the plant wants 220 machines a day.

174
00:14:30,827 --> 00:14:34,101
Find the load at 60 and 68 percent OEE,

175
00:14:34,121 --> 00:14:39,421
the OEE that keeps the load at 90 percent without new machines,

176
00:14:39,441 --> 00:14:42,969
the load with one more unit at 68 percent,

177
00:14:42,989 --> 00:14:48,712
and make a recommendation with the 2.4-minute assembly takt in mind.

178
00:14:49,112 --> 00:14:55,779
Two: an adjustment characteristic has mean 0.070 millimetres, sigma 5 microns,

179
00:14:55,799 --> 00:15:01,351
tolerance 0.04 to 0.10, subgroups of five. Find the X-bar limits;

180
00:15:01,371 --> 00:15:04,523
if the mean drifts 5 microns an hour,

181
00:15:04,543 --> 00:15:09,839
find the chance a single machine fails after 2, 4 and 6 hours,

182
00:15:09,859 --> 00:15:16,612
then choose your run rules, the containment steps, and the first four 8D steps.

183
00:15:17,012 --> 00:15:22,469
Three: write two test.result messages for end-of-line test,

184
00:15:22,489 --> 00:15:28,596
successful trims out of 50 and the low-oil temperature rise. Four:

185
00:15:28,616 --> 00:15:33,609
hook life is Weibull with beta 2.2 and eta 5000 hours;

186
00:15:33,629 --> 00:15:37,786
two machines each run 225 hours next quarter,

187
00:15:37,806 --> 00:15:44,192
with hooks at 1000 and 4000 hours. Find each one's chance of failing.

188
00:15:44,592 --> 00:15:49,752
Submit the assignment and the lab report on the WenQuest platform.

189
00:15:51,272 --> 00:15:55,093
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example:

190
00:15:55,113 --> 00:15:59,937
WQ-LS has four on-off options plus three top-speed grades,

191
00:15:59,957 --> 00:16:06,116
and eight combinations aren't allowed. How many usable variants are there?

192
00:16:06,516 --> 00:16:10,153
Or: with the machining centres as in the lecture,

193
00:16:10,173 --> 00:16:13,212
what is their load at 200 machines a day?

194
00:16:13,612 --> 00:16:19,352
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

195
00:16:20,872 --> 00:16:22,292
Three things to remember.

196
00:16:22,692 --> 00:16:29,818
One: the seven breaks come from information without IDs in one linkable place;

197
00:16:29,838 --> 00:16:35,682
the digital factory has five layers, talks only through the bus,

198
00:16:35,702 --> 00:16:39,072
and needs a person to confirm writes.

199
00:16:39,472 --> 00:16:45,824
Two: X-bar limits are mu-zero plus or minus three sigma over root n;

200
00:16:45,844 --> 00:16:49,385
run rules catch drift before failures,

201
00:16:49,405 --> 00:16:54,164
earlier with more rules but with more false alarms.

202
00:16:54,564 --> 00:16:55,017
Three:

203
00:16:55,037 --> 00:17:00,861
scan lots in at assembly to trace back to causes and forward to the scope.

204
00:17:01,261 --> 00:17:04,036
The slides, the virtual lab,

205
00:17:04,056 --> 00:17:11,024
the assignment and the quiz are all on the WenQuest learning platform,

206
00:17:11,044 --> 00:17:17,712
at learn.wenquestrobotics.com. Sign up for free and start learning.

207
00:17:18,112 --> 00:17:19,452
See you next time.
